  "parts": [
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_a_i",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": [
        "property_identification"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Recall the definition of isotopes in terms of proton number Z and nucleon number A for the same element.",
        "Hint 2: Deduce what must be identical (determined by Z) and what must differ to give different A for P and Q."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: Isotopes have same proton number, so number of protons is the same/equal.",
        "B1: Isotopes have different nucleon numbers, so number of neutrons is different/unequal."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Isotopes are nuclei of the same element with the same proton number $Z$ but different nucleon number $A$.",
        "Since $Z$ is the same, the number of protons is equal for P and Q.",
        "Since $A = Z + N$ differs, with $Z$ constant, the neutron number $N = A - Z$ must be different for P and Q."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Reversing the comparison, stating protons differ and neutrons are the same.",
        "Describing isotopes only as having different masses without specifying protons versus neutrons."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_a_ii",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "property_identification"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Recall that $\\beta^-$ decay is a neutron changing to a proton inside the nucleus, and write the quark make-up of a neutron (udd) and a proton (uud) to see which quark flavour changes."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: A down quark changes to an up quark (udd changes to uud)."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "In $\\beta^-$ decay a neutron converts to a proton.",
        "Neutron quark composition is up down down: $$udd$$",
        "Proton quark composition is up up down: $$uud$$",
        "Therefore one down quark changes to an up quark."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating up changes to down, which is the change for $\\beta^+$ decay, not $\\beta^-$ decay.",
        "Describing only nucleon change (neutron to proton) without giving the required quark level change."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_a_iii",
      "difficulty": 1,
      "question_patterns": [
        "particle_decay_conservation"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Apply conservation of lepton number and energy in $\\beta^-$ decay and recall which nearly massless neutral lepton is emitted with the electron."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "B1: (Electron) antineutrino is also emitted."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Conservation of lepton number, energy and momentum in $\\beta^-$ decay requires a second lepton in addition to the electron.",
        "This particle is the (electron) antineutrino, denoted $\\bar{\\nu}_e$."
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Stating neutrino instead of antineutrino, which would violate lepton number conservation.",
        "Stating gamma photon, confusing beta decay with gamma de-excitation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "part_id": "9702_s23_22_q08_b",
      "difficulty": 2,
      "question_patterns": [
        "direct_calculation"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Hint 1: Recall the quark charges in units of $e$: charm quark is $+2/3e$ and bottom quark is $-1/3e$.",
        "Hint 2: Add the three quark charges together for two charm quarks plus one bottom quark to find the total hadron charge."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "C1: Charge of charm = +2/3e (so 2 charm = +4/3e) and charge of bottom = -1/3e.",
        "A1: Total = +2/3e + 2/3e - 1/3e = +1e."
      ],
      "teacher_walkthrough": [
        "Total charge is the algebraic sum of the constituent quark charges.",
        "Quark charges in terms of $e$: $$Q_{charm} = +\\frac{2}{3}e$$ $$Q_{bottom} = -\\frac{1}{3}e$$",
        "For two charm quarks and one bottom quark: $$Q_{total} = Q_{charm} + Q_{charm} + Q_{bottom}$$",
        "Substitute values: $$Q_{total} = \\left(+\\frac{2}{3}e\\right) + \\left(+\\frac{2}{3}e\\right) + \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}e\\right)$$",
        "Combine fractions: $$Q_{total} = \\frac{2+2-1}{3}e = \\frac{3}{3}e = +1e$$"
      ],
      "common_pitfalls": [
        "Using wrong sign for bottom quark, taking it as +1/3e instead of -1/3e.",
        "Forgetting there are two charm quarks and adding only one charm charge to one bottom charge."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
